{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_adxsiwodoa62jja5elbkq2kfevn6aadfyl4t743eiy2upt346zqa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. All four phases pass under every configuration.\n\n## What the code actually is\n\n`c_src/CMakeLists.txt` builds an **executable**, not a library. The entire C program is:\n\n```c\nint main() {\n    char text[128];\n    while (fgets(text, 128, stdin)) { fputs(text, stdout); }\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\nSo the public surface is a single symbol, `main`. I built the same unmodified source as a `.so` (`gcc -shared -fPIC`, output outside `c_src/`) to get a loadable comparison target, and added a `cdylib` target to the Rust crate exporting `#[no_mangle] extern \"C\" fn main`, so both `.so`s can be dlopen'd side by side.\n\n## Three real divergences found and fixed\n\nThe original translation passed every happy-path echo test — all the bugs were in **stream semantics**:\n\n1. **stdout buffering discipline.** C follows glibc: line-buffered when `isatty(1)`, otherwise 4096-byte block-buffered. The Rust used `LineWriter`, so with stdout on a pipe it echoed each line immediately while C emitted nothing until 4096 bytes accumulated. Rewrote `src/echo.rs` to reproduce glibc's buffering exactly.\n2. **SIGPIPE.** C dies from SIGPIPE (wait status `-13`) on a broken stdout; the Rust runtime sets `SIGPIPE` to `SIG_IGN`, so it exited 0. `src/main.rs` now restores `SIG_DFL`.\n3. **Write errors.** C ignores `fputs`'s return value and drains stdin regardless; the Rust broke out of the loop on the first write error, leaving a shared stdin partially consumed.\n\n## Phase artifacts\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly `{main}`; Rust `.so` exports it, 0 missing, 0 extras, 0 unresolved imports (verified by `ldd -r` *and* a subset check of all 51 imports against the linked libraries' export tables). Nothing was stubbed; no C source was left untranslated.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 15 rows. The C has *no* explicit error handling (one unconditional `return 0`), so the rejection surface is implicit: the conditions making `fgets` return `NULL`, `fputs`'s silent truncation at the first NUL, the 128-byte bound, and ignored write failures. All 15 have passing tests asserting the *same* code/signal, not just \"both failed\".\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 35 rows across 10 axes derived from what the C branches on. All checked.\n\nThe sharpest test is row 34: stdin and stdout are the **same file** with NUL-bearing input, so the output is shorter than the input and the file is rewritten while still being read — the final bytes depend on the exact read/write syscall interleaving. C and Rust produce identical md5s.\n\n## Results\n\n```\n52 tests, 0 failures — stable across 3 consecutive runs\nALL CONFIGURATIONS PASS   (./scripts/verify_all.sh)\n```\n\nBoth feature combinations that exist (`--no-default-features`, `--all-features` — the crate declares none) plus both profiles, since `panic = \"abort\"` makes release a genuinely distinct build.\n\nTwo notes on things I decided rather than assumed: I treated the process interface as the authoritative external API (it's a `main`-only program, so the shell *is* the caller) and used `dlopen` on `main` in forked children with redirected fds as the FFI-level check — rather than testing Rust functions directly. And `c_src/` source is untouched; the only addition there is `c_src/build/`, which the task's own build instructions create."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":703,"cache_read_input_tokens":180563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":703,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55ad7133-91d4-4758-8739-8a336070d8f2","uuid":"5b5cae51-1070-4286-ab48-1d29ef464798","timestamp":"2026-08-17T22:13:25.534Z"}
